bmed it's much further down in the  list these second two records that i get  from esearch are available here the top  record returned by the web is not  returned all right in the top records by  esearch  so these are the kinds of  inconsistencies that  many of you have been reporting like why  is it different where did the record go  um i expected this record and i'm not  seeing it so this is the problem is  trying to address this inconsistency  between these two versions of pubmed  so while this appears to be a bug  uh some kind of malfunction  in fact it's two parallel systems  behaving as expected the problem is  they're two parallel systems so here's  what's happening  before we introduced  the new version of web pubmed in may of  2020  the consistent behavior  between the web and e-utility versions  of pubmed  was  allowed by the fact that there was one  set of data and one index for searching  so there was one set of pubmed data  there was one entree index and again  this is a horribly  oversimplified version  of what happens in pubmed almost  ridiculously simplified but i i hope it  will make the point i'm trying to make  the database for pubmed was indexed in  one entree index if you were a website  user you would explore that entree index  to generate your search results  if you were an api user the e-utilities  would explore and interrogate that exact  same index all right to get search  results so the search results matched  right between the two different  interfaces what happened in 2020 when we  introduced the new version of web pubmed  is that we introduced a solar index  right to support the web version of  pubmed  right so solar is another product that  index the same data  but it's a different search index  from the entree index and so the web  version of pubmed now  uses a solar index of the pubmed data  while the e-utilities are continuing to  use the older entree index  these two indices one are not updated at  the same frequency or at the same