at we've already  discussed the results will be the same  as the web all right so you'll get the  same top ten thousand records  so that's the change to e-search  e-post  and e-fetch have some minor changes that  will be happening epost also will only  accept 10 000 records  maximum per url request  this is actually  something that very few people do  to submit that many records in a single  uh request but ten thousand is now the  the hard maximum  um you can certainly do another batch of  ten thousand that's fine uh and you can  do as many of those as you want but you  can only do ten 000 at a time in one url  for efetch there is a change that i  would say is not trivial but it is a  change that affects very few users  we are not going to be  supporting the asn 1 format as a return  in the new pubmed  so the utilities will no longer return  asn 1  they will be returning xml by default  most people that we can detect  are using a rep mode for something like  at the abstract right or even the xml uh  or one of the other formats very few  users are accessing asm1 directly  it's not something that we are going to  be able to support uh in the new version  of the api  if you do have urls  that do not supply a value of rep mode  and are just using the default  that currently provides asn 1  right in the future it will provide xml  right so  for if there are  those of you who are using that default  efetch return that will change from asm1  to xml  but the really good news is that's it  that's the only thing that's changing  the history server is going to be there  and will will work uh e-link is largely  unchanged we'll just continue to work as  it will  um esummary is largely unchanged it will  continue to work as it will  and none of the urls otherwise are  changing you can use the same parameters  you can use the same syntax  all of that should generate an  equivalent result uh in the new version  the updated version of the api compared  to the current version  so wha